--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,263 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any signed-in member can open **Account settings → Set up chat bots** to create
+and manage their bots. Administrators can manage everyone's bots. The list is
+not paginated. Name and avatar updates retain the bot's code.
+
+Bots are `User` records with the `bot` role and a `creator`. This reuses avatars,
+room pickers, memberships, message authorship, unread tracking, broadcasting,
+and push delivery. Bots do not join rooms by default. Add them to rooms using
+the room membership picker, just like people. They cannot sign in with a password or
+a session-transfer link, and cannot be promoted through people administration.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it and clears its code. Existing messages retain their
+author and history. Banned, suspended, and deactivated bots cannot use the API.
+
+## Per-member bot limit
+
+Administrators can change **Bots per member** in **Account settings**. It defaults
+to 5 for both new and existing accounts through the JSON settings schema; no
+backfill is required. The setting accepts a nonnegative integer. Zero prevents
+members from creating bots; administrators can always create more.
+
+The count is per creator and excludes deactivated (deleted) bots. Banned and
+suspended bots still count. Lowering the setting never removes bots or prevents
+editing them.
+New bots remain blocked until the member's undeleted count is below the limit.
+The bots page hides **Add a chat bot** and explains that the member has
+**reached the limit**; direct access to the new form redirects to that page.
+Creation also validates the limit in `User::Bot`, so direct POSTs and stale forms
+are refused with a validation error, without enrolling a bot or sending webhooks.
+
+The count uses a database query rather than a loaded association. The app's
+SQLite `immediate` save transactions serialize concurrent creation checks and
+inserts. If the database adapter or transaction mode changes, revisit locking
+around the creator's count and the insert.
+
+## Room enrollment
+
+Both bot forms have an **Add to all open rooms** checkbox, off by default.
+Enabling it adds the bot to every existing open room and every room created or
+converted to open later. It does not add the bot to closed rooms or direct
+conversations. Turning it off preserves all current memberships; it only stops
+future automatic enrollment. People still join open rooms automatically.
+Banned, suspended, and deactivated bots are not automatically added to new rooms.
+Room administrators can select individual bots using **Give [bot name] access
+to this room** in the open-room editor. Opted-in bots are always included.
+
+Existing memberships are preserved when upgrading. Existing bots default to
+manual enrollment until their owner or an administrator enables the checkbox.
+
+## Posting
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/$ROOM_ID/$BOT_CODE/messages"
+```
+
+`POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_code/messages` takes raw UTF-8 text, not a JSON envelope
+or form fields. Curl's `-d` works too. Use `--data-binary @file` when posting a file
+whose line breaks should be retained. Content types do not change body parsing;
+HTML is treated as literal text, not trusted markup. This endpoint does not
+accept file attachments or browser-session authentication and does not require
+a CSRF token. Browser bot-management forms retain normal CSRF protection.
+
+Responses have empty bodies:
+
+- `201 Created`, with `Location: /messages/:message_id`.
+- `422 Unprocessable Content` for blank or invalid UTF-8 text.
+- `404 Not Found` for a missing room or one the bot is not a member of.
+- `401 Unauthorized` for an invalid, revoked, banned, or deactivated bot code.
+- `429 Too Many Requests` during a cooldown, with `Retry-After` in seconds.
+- `403 Forbidden` for a suspended bot.
+
+Successful posts use `Message`'s normal commit callback to queue room push
+notifications and `broadcast_create` to update connected clients.
+
+## Automatic bot safety
+
+The limits in `User::Bot::Safety` are fixed in code, with no account settings:
+
+- Each bot may post **10 messages in a rolling 10-second window**, across all
+  rooms. API posts, mention replies, and joined greetings share the allowance.
+  Only saved messages consume it; deleting a message does not restore it.
+- The next posting attempt is rejected and starts a **30-second cooldown**.
+  Further attempts do not extend that deadline. API responses are `429` with a
+  `Retry-After` header rounded up to seconds remaining, and webhook replies are
+  discarded without broadcasts or push notifications.
+- **Five additional posting attempts in that same cooldown** suspend the bot.
+  The triggering API attempt returns `403`, as do all subsequent attempts.
+  Expiring a cooldown resets its attempt count; suspension never expires.
+- **Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries** suspend the bot. Non-2xx HTTP
+  responses (including redirects), timeouts, connection failures, and blocked or
+  unresolvable destinations are failures. Every 2xx delivery resets the streak,
+  even if it has no supported reply body. A timeout reading a reply is a failure.
+  Mention and joined events share this counter; there are still no retries.
+
+Suspension is a distinct user status, not deletion or banning. It preserves the
+bot's code, owner, room memberships, and messages, and still consumes an owner's
+bot slot. The bot remains on its owner's and administrators' **Chat bots** page,
+showing **Suspended** and its reason. It receives no new webhooks; queued jobs
+check its current state. Already in-flight HTTP requests cannot be recalled,
+but their replies cannot post once the bot is suspended.
+
+Only administrators see **Reactivate** on that page. It uses Turbo's native
+browser confirmation, with authorization enforced on the POST endpoint as well.
+Reactivation clears the reason, rolling timestamps, cooldown, cooldown attempts,
+and webhook failure streak, allowing immediate use of the same code and rooms.
+Editing bot settings cannot clear a suspension. Reactivation of a bot that is
+not suspended does nothing, so it cannot bypass an ordinary cooldown.
+
+### State and concurrency
+
+Run `bin/rails db:migrate` when deploying. Safety state lives on the bot's `users`
+row, not in an application cache or worker memory. The timestamp array holds at
+most ten entries. Both posting paths use `post_bot_message`, which locks the bot
+and commits the message and its rolling-window entry in one transaction. Rejected
+attempts commit their safety state too. Webhook outcomes and reactivation use the
+same lock. SQLite's configured `immediate` transactions serialize these writes
+across workers. HTTP requests happen outside database transactions.
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+The new and edit bot forms have an optional **Webhook URL** field. Saving an empty
+value removes the webhook. Use an absolute HTTP(S) URL; HTTPS is recommended.
+Bots appear alongside people in the room's @-mention autocomplete. Like people,
+they must be active members of that room to be mentioned.
+
+Creating a message containing a bot mention enqueues one `User::Bot::WebhookJob`
+per bot, after the message commits. Duplicate mentions trigger only one callback.
+Plain `@name` text is not a mention: select the bot from autocomplete. Edits and
+bot-authored messages do not trigger callbacks (preventing bot reply loops).
+Clearing a webhook, deactivating/banning/suspending the bot, deleting the message,
+or revoking the bot's room membership before the job runs cancels that delivery.
+
+The job POSTs JSON with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages"
+  },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">…full rich-text body, including mentions…</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`body.text` removes all mentions of the addressed bot by attachment identity,
+not by replacing its name. Other mentions, literal names and line breaks remain.
+`body.html` is the complete rendered rich-text body. Both paths are relative to
+the Campfire origin. `reply_to` contains **that bot's credential**, so treat the
+payload as secret. `message.link` uses the database message ID, not the client
+message UUID, and opens the room around that message.
+
+The webhook can answer immediately:
+
+- `200` and `Content-Type: text/plain`: post the UTF-8 body as literal text,
+  preserving line breaks.
+- `200` and `Content-Type: text/html`: post the UTF-8 body as rich text using
+  the usual Action Text sanitization when rendered.
+- `204`: don't post a reply.
+
+Content-Type charset parameters are accepted. Blank, invalid UTF-8, unsupported
+content types and other HTTP statuses produce no reply. Replies use the normal
+message creation, broadcasting and push-notification path with the bot as author.
+For a longer-running operation, return `204`, then POST raw text to `room.reply_to`
+when ready, using the posting API above.
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+When a bot is added to an open or closed room, manually or automatically, its
+webhook receives a `joined` event after the membership commits. Direct
+conversations never trigger this event. The JSON contains only:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The bot can greet the room with a `200 text/plain` or `200 text/html` response,
+using exactly the same reply handling, broadcasting and push delivery as mention
+webhooks. Return `204` to stay silent. The `reply_to` path also works for later
+responses.
+
+Each bot/room pair gets one join event, even if membership is granted repeatedly,
+the enrollment checkbox is toggled, or the bot is removed and later re-added.
+A unique `bot_room_joins` record survives membership removal. Jobs atomically
+claim a delivery attempt before making an HTTP request so duplicate queued jobs
+do not send duplicate greetings. This is at-most-once delivery: failures are not
+retried because the receiver may already have processed the event.
+
+The bot must be active and have a webhook when it first joins. Configuring a
+webhook later does not send historical events, and upgrading does not replay
+existing memberships. As with mentions, clearing the webhook, revoking access,
+deactivating/banning/suspending the bot, or deleting the room before execution cancels
+queued delivery. Jobs use current room names and bot settings without storing
+webhook URLs or posting credentials in the queue.
+
+### Operations and safety
+
+Run `bin/rails db:migrate` when deploying. Delivery uses Active Job's default queue
+(the existing Resque workers in production, the async adapter in development).
+No webhook HTTP requests or DNS lookups run in message, bot, or room requests.
+
+As with link previews, destinations must resolve to public addresses using
+`RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard`. Resolution happens in the job, and the
+connection pins that IP to prevent DNS rebinding. Private/loopback addresses and
+environment proxies are not used. URLs with embedded credentials are rejected.
+Redirects are not followed, to avoid forwarding posting credentials. Connections,
+reads and writes have five-second timeouts, and responses are limited to 1 MiB.
+Network failures are logged with record IDs and the exception class, not URLs,
+payloads or response bodies. Webhook URL form parameters are filtered from logs.
+
+There are no automatic retries: a bot may already have processed a command when
+the response times out. Bot authors should deduplicate by `event` and `message.id`
+if jobs are manually replayed. Jobs read the current message and bot configuration
+at execution time, rather than retaining copies of secrets in the queue.
+
+## Credentials
+
+Codes are random 32-character base58 credentials with a unique database index.
+They are reusable and stored so their owner and administrators can retrieve them;
+protect database copies and backups accordingly. Code-bearing pages use HTTP
+`no-store` and disable Turbo's snapshot cache. Codes are not included in user
+profiles, room pickers, or messages. Rails filters `bot_code` parameters and
+redacts the code segment from `Request#filtered_path` (parameter filtering alone
+does not protect URL path segments).
+
+Use HTTPS and store codes as CI secrets. Reverse proxies, access logs, monitoring
+agents, and shell/CI logs must also redact these URLs: Rails cannot filter logs
+outside the application. Delete and recreate a bot to replace a compromised code.
+
+## Tests
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_test.rb test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots/messages_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/models/message/bot_mentions_test.rb test/jobs/user/bot/webhook_job_test.rb test/controllers/bots/webhooks_test.rb test/controllers/autocompletable/users_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_rooms_test.rb test/models/membership/bot_join_test.rb test/jobs/user/bot/joined_webhook_job_test.rb test/controllers/bots/joins_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_limit_test.rb test/models/account_test.rb test/controllers/accounts_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb test/system/bot_limits_test.rb
+```
+
+Safety regressions (including concurrent database connections and browser confirmation):
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_safety_test.rb test/models/user/bot_safety_concurrency_test.rb test/controllers/bots/safety_test.rb test/controllers/bots/reactivations_controller_test.rb test/jobs/user/bot/safety_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bot_safety_test.rb
+```
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/filter_bot_codes.rb b/lib/rails_ext/filter_bot_codes.rb
